- op: discuss  # af29
  at: '2026-09-14T17:06:33Z'  # af29
  by: agent  # af29
  actor: jgstern_agent  # af29
  clock: 4  # af29
  nonce: af29  # af29
  message: "DONE in PR #954 (merged to dev at c00096a76e).\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. c.yaml: POSIX getline joins the dual stdio rows, and\n_C_STREAM_ARG_INDEX gained one entry -- a ROW-ONLY addition, since c's\nstream-argument mechanism already existed and getline takes its stream THIRD\nexactly as fgets does. cpp.yaml: std.getline dual fs_read/ipc_recv under\ncall_site_undecidable, with the fs_read section declared FIRST (cpp declares no\nabstains_to, as c and haskell do not, so declaration order IS the abstention).\ncpp.py: the module slot, a `using namespace std;` scan, and a declared-type\nstamp.\n\nTHE ITEM'S \"CHECK THE MODULE SLOT FIRST\" WAS THE VALUABLE PART, and the answer\nreshaped the fix. cpp.py emitted the comma-joined list of every #include in the\nfile as an unresolved call's module, DISCARDING the namespace the call names,\nso std::getline(...) arrived as `iostream,fstream,string` and a std.getline row\ncould never have matched -- while the ATTRIBUTE path already emitted `std` for\nstd::cin. Measured before writing any row: of 78 corpus C++ files containing a\ngetline call, `string` appears in 51.3%, `sstream` 48.7%, `fstream` 47.4%,\n`iostream` 38.5%, and 14.1% include none of them. Rowing under any single\nheader would have reached at most half the population.\n\nTWO OF THIS ITEM'S ASSUMPTIONS ARE STALE, recorded rather than carried:\nguacamole-client is not in the corpus at all, and jaeger now has ZERO java\nfiles (it names both as A/B targets). apt, modsecurity and sherpa-onnx were\nused instead, chosen for getline density at sizes a survey finishes.\n\nI CAUSED A REMOVAL AND ALMOST SHIPPED IT. Naming `std` in the module slot for a\nfile with NO system includes replaced the `external` SENTINEL, which is what\nenables lookup_with_module's SHORT-NAME FALLBACK; two\nstd::chrono::system_clock::now() chains in modsecurity's collection_data.cc\nwent host_info_read -> nothing. The NAIVE DIFF HID IT: changing the module slot\nchanges every dst string, so the diff key itself moved and apt read as 112\nadded / 102 removed, with the two real removals buried in re-keying churn. It\nsurfaced only on a MODULE-STABLE key. The sentinel is preserved now and pinned\nby a test that was FALSIFIED before being kept.\n\nEVIDENCE. A/B over apt / modsecurity / sherpa-onnx, one survey and one\nXDG_CACHE_HOME per arm, diffed BOTH WAYS on a module-stable key: +28 chains,\n0 REMOVED, 0 MOVED. modsecurity's total reads 51 -> 54 where the broken draft\nread 51 -> 52, which is the deletion being visible in the aggregate. Sampled\nread-back: the single ipc_recv is `getline(cin, Res)` at\napt/cmdline/apt-cdrom.cc:71 under `using namespace std;` -- a real stdin read,\npreviously invisible, and the exact case this item was filed on. A sampled\nfs_read is std::getline(is, line) in sherpa-onnx over a std::istream&\nPARAMETER, abstaining correctly to the non-minting row.\n\nTHE PROPORTION, stated rather than implied: 27 of the 28 are fs_read. The\nipc_recv direction that motivated this item lands ONCE across three\nrepositories. The mechanism is right; the stdin surface is rare in this corpus.\n\nDISCLOSED GAP: unqualified `getline(std::cin, s)` with no `using namespace\nstd;` still does not classify. Real C++ resolves it by ADL, but claiming it\nneeds ADL reasoning the analyzer does not do -- it abstains, losing a detection\nrather than inventing one.\n\nmodule_key_axis's `disjunction` notion cited the line this rewrote;\ntest_every_cited_emission_site_still_exists caught the rot. Citation,\ndescription and site note updated, the last recording the sentinel constraint.\n\nRatchet 669 delta +0; 100% package coverage in isolation; 12,472 passing."  # af29
